@short_title Configuration & running

{0 Compiling and configuring Eliom modules}



This chapter explains how to set Eliom's
options in the Ocsigen server configuration file, and how to define options for your
Eliom modules. See the Ocsigen server documentation for more information about the
{{!/ocsigenserver/page-config}server's configuration}.

Note, however, that {{!//workflow/page-distillery}Eliom's
distillery} provides templates to get started at once with Eliom!

{1:misc Using Eliom modules with Ocsigen server}

{3 Configuration file}

To run an Ocsigen server with an Eliom module [example.cmo],
add the following lines to the Ocsigen configuration file
([/etc/ocsigenserver/ocsigenserver.conf] most of the time):

{v
<extension findlib-package="ocsipersist-sqlite-config">
  <database file="/var/lib/mysite/ocsidb"/>
</extension>
<extension findlib-package="eliom.server"/>
<host>
  <site path="examples">
    <eliommodule module="/path/to/example.cmo" />
    <eliom />
  </site>
</host>
v}

Eliom stores persistent session data and persistent Eliom references
with Ocsipersist. The first extension loads the SQLite backend of
Ocsipersist, and gives the database file, in an existing directory.
Since ocsipersist 3.2, there is no default location. See
{{!//server/page-state.ocsipersist_config}Configuring the database}
for the other backends.

The block surrounded by the [<site>] tag creates a sub-site on your
host, in directory [examples]. The [<site>] tag is optional if
you want to register your services at the root of the server path tree.

The [<eliommodule>] tag defines an Eliom module to be loaded (dynamically while
starting Ocsigen server) for this subsite.

{3 Running Ocsigen server}

Run Ocsigen server, by typing the command

{v
ocsigenserver
v}

or, if your configuration file is not in the default
location:

{v
ocsigenserver -c <your configuration file>
v}

You should see the pages of your module in site [examples/], for example
at url [http://your_server/examples/<service path>].

{%wodoc:div class="concept"%}{b Debugging}

{b Tip:} Add the option [<debugmode/>] in
the configuration file during the development process of your application.
Thus, Ocsigen server will display the exceptions raised during
the generation of a page in error pages.

{b Tip:} To debug your programs,
add the option [-v] (or [-vv], [-vvv]) of
the [ocsigenserver] command to have verbose information on what happens
during requests. Also have a look at the logs when something goes
wrong: the files of the log directory ([<logdir>]), or syslog
([<syslog>]). Since Ocsigen Server 8.1, there is no default log
directory: without these tags, the logs go to the standard output and
standard error.{%wodoc:end%}


{2 Several Eliom modules for one site}

If your site consists of several modules, you can load them
consecutively from the configuration file using several
[eliommodule]-tags.
Note that there can be only one {%wodoc:span class="code"%}<eliom{%wodoc:end%}>
tag for each {%wodoc:span class="code"%}<site{%wodoc:end%}>
(or {%wodoc:span class="code"%}<host{%wodoc:end%}>), however.

{2 Using findlib with Eliom modules}

If your module has a findlib {%wodoc:span class="code"%}META{%wodoc:end%} file,
it is also possible (and recommended!) to do:

{v
<host>
  <site path="examples">
    <eliommodule findlib-package="package-name" />
    <eliom />
  </site>
</host>
v}



{2:reload Updating sites without shutting down the server}

Ocsigen server has a feature that allows the configuration to be reloaded
without shutting down the server (see section "Reloading the configuration file"
of the {{!/ocsigenserver/page-config}server's configuration}).
This can be used to reload Eliom modules without closing volatile sessions.
To do that, write [reload] into the server's command pipe (the path given
by [<commandpipe>]), for example:

{v
echo reload > /var/run/ocsigenserver_command
v}

Most distributions also provide a command, usually
[/etc/init.d/ocsigenserver reload].

Only modules loaded inside [<host>] or [<site>] will be reloaded.
Modules loaded using [<extension>] or [<library>] will not.

Have a look at the logs to see if all went well during the reload.
If something went wrong, old services may still be reachable.

Warning:
- Services created with the old modules or URLs that have not been
  masked by new ones will still be reachable after the update!
- During the reload, some information of the configuration file will
  not be re-read (for example port numbers, user and group, etc.).


{1 Interacting with Ocsigen server from Eliom programs}

{2 Defining an exception handler for the whole site}

When an exception is raised during the generation of a page, or when
the page has not been found or has wrong parameters, an HTTP error 500
or 404 is sent to the client. You may want to catch these exceptions
to print your own error page.  Do this using
{!val:Eliom.Registration.set_exn_handler}.
Here is an example:

{@ocaml[let _ = Eliom.Registration.set_exn_handler
   (fun e -> match e with
    | Eliom.Common.Eliom_404 ->
        Eliom.Registration.Html.send ~code:404
          (html
             (head (title (txt "")) [])
             (body [h1 [txt "Eliom tutorial"];
                    p [txt "Page not found"]]))
    | e -> Lwt.fail e)
]}

The handler may send any kind of content. In a client-server
application, send the error page with the [send] function of the
application, as in [My_app.send ~code:404 page]: the client-side
program then displays it without leaving the application.

To send a page with an error status from a service handler, for
instance a page about something that does not exist, there is no need
for an exception: call {!val:Eliom.Registration.set_status} in the
service handler, see
{{!//server/services/page-handlers.error_handling}Error handling}.

{2 Giving configuration options to your sites}

You can add your own options in the configuration file for your Web
site. For example:

{v
    <eliommodule module="/path/to/yourmodule.cmo">
      <youroptions> ...
    </eliommodule>
v}

Use {!Eliom.Config.get_config} during the initialization
of your module to get the data between {%wodoc:span class="code"%}<eliommodule{%wodoc:end%}>
and {%wodoc:span class="code"%}</eliommodule{%wodoc:end%}>.

{%wodoc:div class="wip"%}
 Warning: parsing these data is very basic for now.
 That feature will be improved in the future.
{%wodoc:end%}

{2:static_linking Static linking of Eliom modules}

It is possible to link extensions and Eliom modules
{{!/ocsigenserver/page-staticlink}statically}, with or without a
configuration file.

{3 With a configuration file}

The toplevel code of statically linked modules runs when the program
starts, before the configuration file is read. The configuration file
does not load statically linked extensions: it initialises them, with
[<extension name="...">] instead of
[<extension findlib-package="...">]. They take their options as usual.
For example, for the SQLite backend of Ocsipersist and for Eliom:

{v
<extension name="ocsipersist">
  <database file="/var/lib/mysite/ocsidb"/>
</extension>
<extension name="eliom"/>
v}

For Eliom modules, this is not straightforward: service
registration and option settings must be delayed until the
configuration file is read. To create a statically linkable Eliom
module, use function
{!Eliom.Service.register_eliom_module}.
It takes as parameters the name of the module and the initialization
function that will be called once the module is initialized in the
configuration file. That function should register the module services
(and possibly call {!Eliom.Config.get_config} if the module has
configuration options).

{%wodoc:div class="wip"%}How to make this easier to use?{%wodoc:end%}

To initialize the module from the configuration file, use the syntax:

{v
<eliommodule name="name"> ... </eliommodule>
v}

(or {%wodoc:span class="code"%}<eliom name="{e name}"> ... </eliom> {%wodoc:end%})
which is equivalent to:

{v
<eliommodule module="name.cmxs"> ... </eliommodule>
v}

(or {%wodoc:span class="code"%}<eliom module="{e name.cmxs}"> ... </eliom> {%wodoc:end%})

with the exception that it does not load the module using {%wodoc:span class="code"%}Dynlink{%wodoc:end%}, but calls the initialization function.{%html:<br/>%}

You can use functions such as
{!Eliom.State.create_volatile_table}
that need information about the site (here, volatile tables are
associated to a site), only during a request or during the
initialization phase of the server.

If you want to use this kind of function before the initialization
phase, for example if your module is linked statically with the
server, you must call these functions using function
{!Eliom.Service.register_eliom_module}.
(One solution is to use a lazy value to delay the creation of the
table, and force that value from the registration function).

{3:no_config_file Without a configuration file}

A program can also start the server itself, without a configuration
file, with {!/ocsigenserver/Ocsigen.Server.start} (see
{{!/ocsigenserver/page-staticlink}Using Ocsigen Server as a library}).
The instruction {!Eliom.App.run} runs your Eliom application in a host
or a site, and its optional parameters replace the options of the
[<eliom>] tag. The services can then be registered by the toplevel code
of your modules.

As there is no configuration file, the program must also give the
location of the Ocsipersist database, with module [Ocsipersist_settings]
(see {{!//server/page-state.ocsipersist_config}Configuring the database}).
Since Ocsigen Server 8.1, without [~logdir] (nor [~syslog_facility]),
the logs go to the standard output and standard error. For example,
with the SQLite backend of Ocsipersist:

{@ocaml[
let () =
  Ocsipersist_settings.set_db_file "/var/lib/mysite/ocsidb";
  Ocsigen.Server.start ~logdir:"/var/log/mysite"
    [ Ocsigen.Server.host
        [Staticmod.run ~dir:"/var/www/mysite" (); Eliom.App.run ()] ]
]}

The template [app] of {{!//workflow/page-distillery}Eliom's
distillery} builds such a program.


{2:extensions Advanced use: create an extension for the server that accesses Eliom's data}

If you want an Ocsigen extension with access to Eliom's data (for
example if you want an extension that will register some services),
you can use the function
{!Eliom.Extension.register_eliom_extension}
to register the function that will generate the
{!/ocsigenserver/Ocsigen.Extensions.type-answer}.

{%wodoc:div class="wip"%}Add more details about this{%wodoc:end%}

{1:config Global configuration options}

Here are Eliom's options you can use in configuration files.

{2 Timeouts}

{%wodoc:div class="wip"%}Review the timeouts for client processes and groups{%wodoc:end%}

State timeouts can be set either inside tag
[<extension findlib-package="eliom.server"/>] (default value
for all sites), or inside a [<eliom/>] tag (default for one site).

Timeouts can also be modified programmatically using functions like
[Eliom.State.set_global_volatile_state_timeout], but by default these
functions will not override configuration files.
(see module {!Eliom.State} for other functions).
Thus, a Web site can set its own defaults and the user can still
override them from the configuration file.  If you want to set a
timeout programmatically even if it has been modified in a
configuration file, use the optional parameter
[~override_configfile:true].

Timeouts can be set either for all scope hierarchies, for one precise
hierarchy, or for the default scope hierarchy.  To do this
programmatically, use the parameter [~cookie_scope] of the
[set_global_*] functions (one hierarchy), or the [set_default_global_*]
functions (all the hierarchies without a value of their own).
In a configuration file, use the optional attribute
[hierarchyname] (where an empty string value means default hierarchy).
If this attribute is absent, the timeout will affect all
states for which no other default has been set.

The level attribute can take values [session] (browser)
or [clientprocess] (tab).

- [<volatiletimeout value="30" [hierarchyname=""] [level="session"]/>]
  The default timeout for
  volatile (in memory) states (value in seconds).
  [value="infinity"] means that the state will
  never expire.
  Note that an Eliom module can set its own default, which the
  configuration file overrides (unless the module uses
  [~override_configfile:true]).
- [<persistenttimeout value="7200"/>]
  Idem for persistent state data
- [<datatimeout value="30"  [hierarchyname=""] [level="session"]/>]
     Like [<volatiletimeout>], but for in memory data
     states only
     (not service states).
- [<servicetimeout value="30" [hierarchyname=""] [level="session"]/>]
  Like [<volatiletimeout>], but for service states only
     (not in memory data states).

{2 Garbage collector of states and services}

These options can appear inside tag
[<extension findlib-package="eliom.server"/>].
For now, it cannot be set for each site independently
(tell us if you need that).

- [<sessiongcfrequency value="30"/>]
     Time between two garbage
     collections of sessions, in seconds (default
     1200). The value ["infinity"] means no GC of session.
- [<persistentsessiongcfrequency value="86400"/>]
     Time between two
     garbage collections of persistent sessions,
     in seconds (default 86400.). The value ["infinity"] means
      no GC of session.
- [<servicesessiongcfrequency value="3600"/>]
     Like [<sessiongcfrequency>],
     but for service sessions only
- [<datasessiongcfrequency value="3600"/>]
     Like [<sessiongcfrequency>],
     but for "in memory data" sessions only

{2 Comet}

This option can appear inside tag
[<extension findlib-package="eliom.server"/>].

- [<comettimeout value="20"/>]
     Time after which the server answers a Comet request that waits for
     data with no data, in seconds (default 20). The client then sends a
     new request. Keep it below the timeouts of the proxies between
     clients and the server.

{2:limits Limiting the number of sessions or coservices}

To fight denial of service, Eliom limits the number of sessions
and the number of dynamic coservices. Without these limitations, it would
be possible for an attacker to open repeatedly lots of sessions,
or to create new services (for example CSRF safe coservices can create lots
of coservices when you reload repeatedly a page). When the limit is reached,
it is still possible to open a new session or create a new service, but the
oldest sessions or services disappear (the ones that have not been used
for the longest time).

{3 Limiting sessions}
First of all, there is a limitation of the number of sessions in a
session group. A typical use is when a user opens several
sessions from several computers. All the sessions belong to the same
group (the group name is usually the user name). The limit is 50 sessions
per group by default, for all kinds of sessions (service sessions,
volatile and persistent data sessions); the tab sessions of a browser
session are limited in the same way, 100 by default. For persistent
sessions, the implementation is not very efficient for very large limits.

It is highly recommended to use session groups when possible.

If you can't use session groups, the volatile sessions (service sessions
and data sessions) that are not in a group are counted together, for the
whole site. This limit is larger: 1 million by default. When it is reached,
the session without a group used least recently is closed, be it the
session of an attacker or of a regular user.

Some figures: if 1 session takes 1000 bytes (data + tables etc),
1 million sessions take 1 GB. If somebody opens 1000 sessions per second,
it takes 1000 s (16 minutes) to reach the limit; while the attack lasts,
the regular users without a group have their sessions closed after 1000 s
of inactivity. The sessions in a group are not affected. To stop such an
attack, limit the rate of requests by client address in front of the
server (reverse proxy). Each browser session may also hold up to 100 tab
sessions by default, which take memory too.

For persistent sessions, there is no limitation without a group.
1 billion sessions take 1 TB. If somebody opens 1000 sessions per second,
then it will take 1 million s (16000 minutes = 266 h = 11 days)
to reach 1TB.

{3 Limiting services}

The number of anonymous coservices is limited by session, and for the
whole site if the service is registered in the global table during a
request. Default values: 1000 for one session, and 500000 for the site.
The anonymous coservices registered in the global table during the
initialisation of the site are not limited.

Note that there is no limitation of named coservices or regular services.
It is not a good practice to allow the creation of too many services
of these kinds dynamically.


{3 How to set limits}

The limits can be set programmatically by each module
(for example to adapt the values to the size of session data) or in the
configuration file
(for instance, to adapt the values to the size of memory)
(see module  {!Eliom.State}).
By default, functions like
[Eliom.State.set_default_max_volatile_sessions_per_group]
will not override a value set in the configuration file
(unless you use [~override_configfile:true]).
Thus, a Web site can set its own defaults and the user can still
override them from the configuration file.

The configuration file options can be set either inside the tag
[<extension findlib-package="eliom.server"/>]
(global configuration), or inside the [<eliom/>] tag
(configuration for each site). But the limits always are for one
site (that is: a global limit value of 10 means 10 for each Eliom site).

The syntax is:
- [<maxvolatilesessionspergroup value="10"/>]
- [<maxservicesessionspergroup value="10"/>]
- [<maxdatasessionspergroup value="10"/>]
- [<maxpersistentsessionspergroup value="10"/>]
- [<maxvolatiletabsessionspergroup value="10"/>]
- [<maxservicetabsessionspergroup value="10"/>]
- [<maxdatatabsessionspergroup value="10"/>]
- [<maxpersistenttabsessionspergroup value="10"/>]
- [<maxvolatilesessionswithoutgroup value="50000"/>]
- [<maxservicesessionswithoutgroup value="50000"/>]
- [<maxdatasessionswithoutgroup value="50000"/>]
- [<maxvolatilegroupspersite value="500000"/>]
- [<maxanonymouscoservicespersession value="1000"/>]
- [<maxanonymouscoservicespersite value="500000"/>]

{2 Application script attributes}

One can choose to set the [defer] or [async] attributes of the
script element referencing the application code, so that HTML
rendering is not blocked while the script is downloaded.

The syntax is:
- [<applicationscript defer="true" async="false"/>]

Both attributes are optional and default to [false].

{2:wasm WebAssembly support}

Eliom applications can be compiled to WebAssembly using [wasm_of_ocaml].
When enabled, the server generates a detection script that automatically
loads the WASM version if the browser supports WebAssembly, with fallback
to JavaScript otherwise.

The syntax is:
- [<wasm enabled="true"/>]

WebAssembly support is disabled by default for backward compatibility.
The configuration file of the Eliom template [app] enables it.

It can also be enabled programmatically using
[Eliom.Config.set_enable_wasm true] or via the [~enable_wasm]
parameter of {!Eliom.App.run}.

For production, use [--profile release] to bundle all WASM modules into a single file,
avoiding multiple HTTP requests at startup.

{2 Serving global data through a distinct URL}

When an Eliom program is initiated client-side, it needs to receive
some global data from the server (that are persistent from request to
request). This data is normally included in the HTML page sent by the
server. Alternatively, it can be served through a separate
request. This is more efficient as it allows this state to be
serialized by the server only once, and cached by the clients (or
possibly a CDN if your server is running behind one), but the client
will receive stale data if global data changes over time.

The syntax to activate this option is the following:
- [<cacheglobaldata path="path" cache="604800"/>]

All attributes are optional. The [path] option can be used to
indicate the path below which the JavaScript file providing the global
data is served. The default path is the empty path. This script name
is the MD5 checksum of its contents (with a [.js] suffix). Thus,
it can be safely cached and is unlikely to conflict with any other
service. The caching duration, in seconds, is set using the
[cache] attribute. It defaults to zero (no caching).

When the application script defer attribute is set (see just above),
global data are also loaded in a deferred way.

{1 Per site configuration options}

{2 Disabling XHR-links}

By default, all links ([a], [form]) of an Eliom application are performed
by Eliom's [change_page] machinery, unless they are created with the
[~xhr:false] option.

You can change this default by adding the attribute [xhr-links] to
the content of the configuration of the Eliom module:
{v
  <eliom ... xhr-links="no" />
v}
Then, all links will perform a normal HTTP request unless explicitly
created with the [~xhr:true] option.

This value can also be set through function
{!Eliom.Config.set_default_links_xhr}.
